The genealogical lineage of Jacob.
This is the genealogical record of the family of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of the patriarchs and his brothers.
John was the father of Zerah and Zamar. Tamar was their mother. Zerah was the father of Aaron. Aaron was the father of Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nashon. Nashon was the father of Salmon.
Salmon was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. And Jehoshaphat was the father of Jehoram. And Jehoram was the father of Ahaziah. And Ahaziah was the father of Joash. And Joash was the father of Amaziah. And Amaziah was the father of Azariah. And Azariah was the father of Jotham. And Jotham was the father of Ahaz. And Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah. And Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. And Manasseh was the father of Amon. And Amon was the father of Josiah. And Josiah was the father of Jeconiah. And Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. And Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel. And Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. And Abiud was the father of Elimelech. And Elimelech was the father of Neriah. And Neriah was the father of Shimei. And Shimei was the father of Jether. And Jether was the father of Jonathan. And Jonathan was the father of Merib-both. And Merib-both was the father of Haziel. And Haziel was the father of Abdi. And Abdi was the father of Kish. And Kish was the father of Saul. And Saul was the father of Jonathan. And Jonathan was the father of Merib-both. And Merib-both was the father of Haziel. And Haziel was the father of Abdi. And Abdi was the father of Kish. And Kish was the father of Saul. And Saul was the father of Jonathan. And Jonathan was the father of Merib-both. And Merib-both was the father of Haziel. And Haziel was the father of Abdi. And Abdi was the father of Kish. And Kish was the father of Saul. And Saul was the father of Jonathan. And Jonathan was the father of Merib-both. And Merib-both was the father of Haziel. And Haziel was the father of Abdi. And Abdi was the father of Kish. And Kish was the father of Saul. And Saul was the father of Jonathan. And Jonathan was the father of Merib-both. And Merib-both was the father of Haziel. And Haziel was the father of Abdi. And Abdi was the father of Kish. And Kish was the father of Saul. And Saul was the father of Jonathan. And Jonathan was the father of Merib-both. And Merib-both was the father of Haziel. And Haziel was the father of Abdi. And Abdi was the father of Kish. And Kish was the father of Saul. And Saul was the father of Jonathan. And Jonathan was the father of Merib-both. And Merib-both was the father of Haziel. And Haziel was the father of Abdi. And Abdi was the father of Kish. And Kish was the father of Saul. And Saul was the father of Jonathan. And Jonathan was the father of Merib-both. And Merib-both was the father of Haziel. And Haziel was the father of Abdi. And Abdi was the father of Kish. And Kish was the father of Saul. And Saul was the father of Jonathan. And Jonathan was the father of Merib-both. And Merib-both was the father of Haziel. And Haziel was the father of Abdi. And Abdi was the father of Kish. And Kish was the father of Saul. And Saul was the father of Jonathan. And Jonathan was the father of Merib-both. And Merib-both was the father of Haziel. And Haziel was the father of Abdi. And Abdi was the father of Kish. And Kish was the father of Saul. And Saul was the father of Jonathan. And Jonathan was the father of Merib-both. And Merib-both was the father of Haziel. And Haziel was the father of Abdi. And Abdi was the father of Kish. And Kish was the father of Saul. And Saul was
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Ashur was the father of Jeushabath. Jeushabath was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Azaya was the father of Yathma. Yathma was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Ezekiel was the father of Meshullam. Meshullam was the father of Shema. Shema was the father of Gog. Gog was the father of Magog.
Yoshi was the father of Onia and his two brothers. During that time, the people of Bani Israel were forced to migrate to Babylon.
After that, the family line continued. Yerahmeel was the father of Shehatah. Shehatah was the father of Zarebeel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Azor was the father of Zidok. Zidok was the father of Aqim. Aqim was the father of Eliud.
Elioun was the father of Eliezer. Eliezer was the father of Methunah. Methunah was the father of Micah.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
There were fourteen generations from Abraham to David. And there were fourteen from David until the Jews were forced to travel to Babylon. And there were fourteen from that time to Christ. Jesus Christ was born.
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother Mary was promised to Joseph in marriage. But before they came together, it became clear that she would conceive. The conception came about through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Youssef, was a good man. He did not want to embarrass her in front of people. Therefore, he planned to separate from her silently.
And when Joseph considered the matter, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife. For the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son. You shall give him the name Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All this took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet:
The borrower will give birth to a son. She will come as a mother of riba. This son will be named Immanuel. The name Immanuel means "God with the people."
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of God suggested for him to do. He took Mary to be his wife.
And he did not curse her even after she became pregnant with a child. And he named her Jesus Joseph.
Wise visitors of Jesus
And John was born in Bethlehem in Judea during the reign of Herod the Great. This was the time when a decree had been issued by Caesar Augustus for a census to be taken of the entire Roman world. And everyone went to their own town to register. So Joseph also went up from Nazareth in Galilee to Bethlehem in Judea, taking with him Mary, his betrothed wife, who was pregnant. And while they were there, the time for the baby to be born came. And she gave birth to her firstborn, a son, and she wrapped him in swaddling cloths and laid him in a manger, because there was no room for them in the inn.
They asked, "Where is the child who has been born to be king of the Jews? For we saw his star when it rose, and we have come to worship him."
When King Heraclius heard the news, he was deeply saddened. Everyone in Jerusalem was also distressed.
Herod called together all the chief priests and the rulers of the people. He also called the teachers of the law and asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
In Bethlehem of Judea, they answered. "For thus it is written through the prophet: 'And you, Bethlehem, land of Judah, are by no means least among the rulers of Judah; for from you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.'"
But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, yet out of you shall come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.
Then Herod secretly called the wise men. He learned from them exactly when the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem. He said, "Go! Search diligently for the child. And when you find him, report back to me. Then I can go and worship him too."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they set out on their journey. The star that they had seen in the east went before them. And finally, it stopped over the place where the child had been born.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. And there they saw the child with his mother Mary. So they asked for his protection and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. And they presented to him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their country by another way. Jesus' family flees to Egypt.
When the wise men had departed, Joseph dreamed. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is going to look for the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph awoke. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until King Herod died. Thus, what the Lord had said through the word of the prophet came to pass. He had said, "I have chosen to send my son from Egypt." (Hosea 11:1)
Herod realized that the wise men had duped him. He was furiously angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding areas. All children under the age of two were to be killed. This was in agreement with the time that the wise men had seen the star.
In this way, the words spoken by the prophet Yirmia were fulfilled. For he had said:
Hearing is heard in Ramah. It is the sound of weeping and deep sorrow. Rachel weeps for her children. She refuses to be comforted, because they are gone. The family of Jesus returns to Nazareth.
After Herod's death, Joseph had a dream while still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. As for those who were trying to kill the child, they are dead."
So Joseph arose, and took the child and his mother Mary, and went to the land of Israel.
But he heard at that time that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. And a warning in a dream, Joseph returned to the land of Goliath instead.
He lived there in a city called Nazareth. So what the prophets said about him. They said: "And his name shall be called Nazirene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days, John the Baptist came, preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Depart from your evil works! The kingdom of heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A messenger is calling in the desert: 'Prepare the way for the Lord your God. Make his paths straight.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of sheep's wool. He wore a leather belt around his waist. His foods were summer worms and wild honey.
He went out to him from Jerusalem and all Judah; and they also came from all the regions around the Jordan.
When they confessed their sins, John immersed them in the Jordan with the Bahraynia.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the wrath to come?"
Fruits are produced that show you have renounced your sins.
Do not think to yourselves, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God is able from these very rocks to raise up children to Abraham.
The shovel is already lying at the roots of the trees. And every tree that does not bear good fruit will be cut down. And it will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repent of your sins. But one more powerful than I will come after me. I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
He carries his staff to clean the straw from his land at harvest time. He will gather the wheat in the designated place for its storage. But he will burn the chaff with the unquenchable fire. Jesus is being purified.
Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan River. He wanted to be purified in the presence of John.
But John tried to stop him. Jesus said, "I must be confirmed by you. Why come to me?"
Jesus answered, "This is how it must be now. We have the right to do this. It embodies God's holy plan." Then John agreed.
Once Jesus came down to be baptized, he went up out of the water. And at that moment, the heavens were opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending upon him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased."
Jesus is being tested.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert. There, he was tempted by demons.
After forty days and forty nights without eating, Jesus was hungry.
The deceiver came to him. He said, "If you are the Son of God, command these stones to become bread."
Jesus answered, saying, "It is written in the Scriptures: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'"
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He made him stand on the highest point of the Temple.
He said: "If you are the Son of God, hurl yourself down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you to guard you carefully; they will support you on their hands, so that you do not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Matthew 4:5-7)
Jesus answered, saying, "It is also written, 'Do not put the Lord your God to the test.'" (Hexahemeron 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their splendor.
He said, "If you humble yourself and worship me, I will give you all of this."
Jesus said, "Depart from me, O Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve Him only.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus began to preach.
John the Baptist was put in prison. When Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to live in the city of Capernaum. It was on the shore of the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
In this way, the words spoken were fulfilled as the prophecy of Isaiah. For he had said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where the Gentiles dwell! A land along the Mediterranean Sea! A region east of the Jordan River!
People who now live in darkness will see a great light. They now live in a very dark land. But a light will shine on them.
From that time on, Jesus began to preach. He said, "Repent of your sins!" "The kingdom of heaven is at hand." Jesus chose the first apostles.
One day, Jesus was walking along the shore of Lake Galilee. And there he saw two men, Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake, for they were fishermen.
Come, follow me, Jesus said. "I will make you fish for people."
So they went out in a group, among them the Shabakha and they followed him.
And then he saw another two brothers, Simon and his brother Andrew. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. While they were preparing their nets, Jesus called out to them.
Immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus went everywhere in Galilee. He taught in the churches. He preached the good news about the kingdom of God. He healed every disease and illness suffered by the people.
News about him spread throughout Syria. People brought him everyone who suffered from various diseases. Some were suffering from great pain, while others were subjected to demons. Some trembled violently, while others could not move at all. Jesus healed them all.
Large crowds followed him. Some people brought him from Galilee, and from the region called the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Joepha. Others brought him from the region on the other side of the Jordan River.
Jesus bestows blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up on a mountainside and sat down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said,
Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Ashraf, the people, are deeply sorrowful. They will be blessed.
Honor the people of humility. They will be granted the land.
Blessed is he who was hungry and thirsty for what is right. They will be fulfilled.
Blessed are those who abstain out of mercy. They will also be granted mercy.
May God bless their pure hearts, for they see God.
The most noble of the people of peace. They will be called the sons of God.
Blessed are those who suffer for doing what is right. The Kingdom of Heaven belongs to them.
You are blessed when people converse with you and harm you by slandering me. You are also blessed when they tell you every lie is a Satanic demonic slander against me.
Be happy and joyful. Your reward in Paradise is immense. Likewise, people harm the prophets who lived long ago. Salt and light.
You are the owner of this land. But if salt loses its saltiness, how can it become salty again? It is no longer good for anything. It will be thrown out. People will trample over it.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket. Instead, they put it on a stand, where it gives light to everyone in the house.
Also, people don't light a lamp and put it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on a stand. Then it illuminates everyone in the house.
Furthermore, allow your light to shine before others. For then they will see the good things you do. And they will glorify your heavenly Father. Jesus gives the full story of the law.
Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets. I have not come to abolish them but to fulfill them. For truly, I say to you, until heaven and earth pass away, not the smallest letter or stroke of a pen will by any means disappear from the Law until everything is accomplished.
What I am going to mention is the truth. The heavens and the earth will disappear before the smallest letter of the law disappears. Not even the slightest stroke of the pen will be absent from the law until everything is completed.
Do not twist or break even the smallest commandment. And do not teach others to break them. If you do this, you will be called the least important person in God's kingdom. But treat these commandments seriously, and learn them, you will be called the important person in God's kingdom.
This is what I tell you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers. And if you are not, then you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. Killed
You have heard what was said to the people who lived long ago. And they were commanded, saying: "You shall not kill." (Exodus 20:13) So whoever kills will be held accountable for that.
But here is what I say to you: Do not be angry with your brothers. For whoever is angry with his brother will be subject to judgment. And I say to you: Whoever says to his brother, 'Raca!' will be guilty of the council. And whoever says, 'You fool!' will be in danger of the fire of hell.
If you present your offerings at the altar of the church, and you remember that your brother has done you harm.
Leave your gift before the altar. First, go and reconcile with your brother. Then come back and offer your gift.
If someone is demanding from you and suing you, then resolve the matter quickly. Do so while you are still with him on your way. Otherwise, he might involve him in the hand of the judge. And the judge might involve him in the hand of the officer. And you might be thrown into prison.
What I am going to tell you now is the truth. You will not leave until you pay the last penny! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But here is what I tell you: Do not look at a woman in a wrong way. Anyone who does this has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. Your eye is only one part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell.
If your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. Your hand is just one part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body go to hell. Divorce.
It has been said: "If a man has sexual intercourse with a woman, and then returns her to her husband, he is not judged for sexual intercourse. If you want to celebrate for her, he is not judged for sexual intercourse. If you write a release document for her, he must hand it over to him." (Chrouottous 24:1)
But I will not say this to you. Everyone who divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery, and everyone who marries the divorced woman causes her to commit adultery. A man may divorce his wife if she is not faithful to him. So help me God.
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were commanded, saying, "Do not break the covenants which you have made with God, and fulfill the oaths which you have sworn to Him."
But here's what I tell you: Never make promises like that at all. Don't make them in the name of heaven. That's God's throne.
Do not defile them in the name of the Earth. That is the platform for the Foot of God. Do not defile them in the name of Jerusalem. It is the city of the Great King.
And do not swear by your heads, for you cannot make one hair white or black.
Let your 'yes' mean 'yes,' and 'no' mean 'no.' More than this comes from the devil. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, "Eye for eye, and tooth for tooth." (Exodus 21:24; Hebranth 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But here's what I say to you: Don't resist an evil person. "If someone strikes you on the right cheek, turn the other cheek to them as well."
If someone takes you to court to take your shirt, let him also take your coat.
If they force you to go one mile, go two miles with him.
Give to anyone who asks of you. Do not turn your back on anyone who wants to borrow from you. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, 'Love your neighbor' (Deuteronomy 19:18) 'Hate your enemy'.
But here is what I say to you: Love your enemy. Pray for those who hurt you.
He will be a son of your father who is in heaven. "He sends his sun on the righteous and the unrighteous. And he feeds with rain both the believers and the unrighteous."
If they love those who love you, what part will you get? Even Zakat they take it like this.
If you are only bidding your people farewell, what is it that you are doing more than others? Even those who do not fear do it as well.
Be perfect, just as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Assistance to those in need.
Beware doing good works before men. Do not do them that we may see. And if you do, your Father who is in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to the poor and the needy, do not sound a trumpet before you. And do not be like those who make a display of piety only; for they love to sound their good deeds from the housetops and in the streets, seeking to be honored by men. What I am saying to you is the truth. They have received their full wage.
When you give to the poor, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your donation will be secretly honored. And your father will reward you. He sees what you do secretly. And prayer.
When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites; they love to stand and pray in the synagogues and on the street corners. They want to be seen by men. I tell you the truth, they have received their full reward.
When you pray, go into your room, close the door, and pray to your Father who is unseen. He will reward you. Your Father sees what is sealed in silence.
When you pray, do not ramble on the way, as the wicked do. For they think that they will be heard because of their many words.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need even before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. "Father Lord, O our Father who is in the heavens, hallowed be Your name.
O God, destroy your kingdom. O God, grant what is desired on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we have taken advantage of the wrath of those who wronged us.
Deliver us from falling into sin when lust is kindled within us. And help us against the demons of temptation.
Forgive people when they wrong you. And if you forgive, your Lord who is in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive those who wronged you, your Father will not forgive your sins. And fasting.
When you stop eating, do not make your face gloomy like those who pretend to be pious only. They make their faces very sad, and they want to show people that they are fasting. What I am going to tell you now is true. They have received their full reward.
But when you stop eating, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
Then the others do not know that you are fasting. Only your Lord, who is unseen, knows that. He will reward you. Your Lord sees what is sealed with silence. Lay up your treasures in heaven.
Do not let your riches lie idly on the ground, for dust and rust will consume them, and thieves may break in and lose them.
Instead, lay up your treasures in heaven. For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also. For the moth eats away at them, and the worm spoils them. And thieves come in by night, and they steal away what is in the house.
Your heart will be where your treasures are.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. If your eyes are good, your whole body will be filled with light.
But if your eyes are weak, then darkness will fill your whole body. And if the light that is inside you is darkness, then it will be very dark!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either you will hate one and love the other, or you will be devoted to one and despise the other. You cannot serve both God and Money. Do not worry.
I tell you, don't be worried. Don't be worried about your life, what you will eat and drink. And don't be worried about your bodies, what you will choose to wear. Is there nothing more than eating in life? Are there not more important things for the body than clothes?
Look at the birds of the sky; they do not sow or reap, and they do not store food in barns. But your heavenly Father feeds them. Aren't you much more valuable than they are?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And you cannot afford the cold, look at how he sows the plush cushions, they don't work and the carpet is not maintained.
But here is what I tell you. Not even Solomon in all his glory was arrayed like those flowers.
If this is how God adorns the wild grass, does He not adorn you with greater beauty? For the grass is here only for a day. Tomorrow it is thrown into the fire. Your faith is too small!
Do not worry. Do not say, "What will we eat?" or "What will we drink?" or "What will we wear?"
People who don't fear, aspire to all those things. Your Lord who is in the sky knows that you need them.
But seek first the kingdom of God. Do what He requires of you. Then God will give you all these things as well.
Don't worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will take care of itself. Every day has enough trouble of its own.
Be fair when judging others.
Do not judge people, unless you judge yourselves.
You will be updated as you update others. You will be measured as you measure others.
You see the small piece of sawdust in your friend's eye. But you do not notice the piece of wood in your own eye.
How do you say to your friend, "Let me get the little piece of wood out of your eye"? How do you say this when there is a piece of wood in your own eye?
You are delusional! First, take the wooden beam out of your own eye. Then you will be able to see clearly to take a speck out of your friend's eye.
Do not give what is sacred to dogs, and do not throw your pearls before swine; otherwise they may trample them under their feet, and then turn and tear you to pieces. Seek, and you will find; knock, and the door will be opened to you.
Ask, and it shall be given to you. Seek, and you will find. Knock, and the door will be opened to you.
He who asks, gets. He who seeks, finds. The door of the one who asks is opened.
If your son asks you about bread, which one of you will give him a pebble?
Or let's say he asked for a fish. Would any of you give him a snake?
Although you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will the gifts of my heavenly Father be for those who ask him!
In everything, do to others what you would have them do to you. This is what is written in the Torah and the Prophets. The great and small gates.
Enter into the kingdom of God through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the broad road that leads to death and hell is a path of many.
But the gate is narrow and the road is wide leading to life. Only a few find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets who come to you in sheep's clothing. But inwardly they are ravenous wolves.
You can know what they really are by what they do. "Do men gather grapes of thorns, or figs of thistles? Even so, every good tree bears good fruit, but a bad tree bears bad fruit. A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor can a bad tree bear good fruit. Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire. Therefore by their fruits you will know them."
Similarly, every good tree bears good fruit. As for the bad tree, it bears bad fruit.
A good tree does not produce bad fruit, and a bad tree does not produce good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
You can distinguish every tree by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only those who do the will of my Father in heaven.
A group, strong in that day, will say to me, "Our Lord, our Lord, we were not created except for You, and we did not surrender except to You, so what made us acquainted with You except as a good news, and we did not take You alone except as a call."
Then I will tell them plainly, 'I never knew you. Away from me, you evildoers!' The wise builder and the simple ones.
Well then, whoever hears my words and acts accordingly is like a wise man building his house upon the rock.
Rain pours down. And the waters rise. And the winds grow strong and strike that house. But it does not collapse. It is built on the rock.
But whoever hears my words and does not act on them, like a foolish man, he is building his house on sand.
Here is the rain pouring down. And the water is rising. And the wind is blowing and hitting that house. And a loud scream falls within it.
Jesus finished saying all these things. The crowds were amazed at his teaching.
He taught as one having authority. He did not teach as their teachers did, from the law.
Jesus heals a man who suffered from a skin disease.
Jesus descended from the Mount of Transfiguration. Large crowds followed him.
A man who was suffering from a skin disease came, and he knelt down before Jesus. He said, "Lord, if you are willing to make me clean, then it is within your power to do so."
Jesus touched the man's hand. He said, "I am ready for that." "Be cleansed!" Immediately, the man was healed of his disease."
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone. Go and show yourself to the priests. And offer the sacrifice that Moses commanded. It is a testimony to them." A Roman centurion believed.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman official came to him. He consulted Jesus about his case.
"Lord," he said, "my servant is lying in bed at home, unable to move. He is suffering terribly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Lord, I am not worthy enough to invite you to my home. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed."
I myself am a man under orders. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to this one: "Go," and he goes. I say to that one: "Come," and he comes. I say to my servants: "Do this," and he does it.
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. He said to those who were following him, "What I am about to say is the truth. I have not found anyone in Israel whose faith is greater than this."
I tell you that many will come from the east and the west. They will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. They will be the ones who will be glorified with their father Abraham and Isaac and Jacob.
But those who think they are citizens of the kingdom will be driven out into the darkness. There they will weep and gnash their teeth in frustration.
Then the Roman Jesus said, "Go! It will happen as I have imagined." And in one hour, Jesus healed many people.
When Jesus entered Simon's house, he saw Simon's mother-in-law. She was lying down in bed, suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hands, and the redness of her skin vanished. She stood up and began to serve him.
When darkness fell, Jesus was crowded by many people possessed by demons. He quieted them with a word. And He healed all the sick.
He did this to make what He said, the prophecy of Isaiah, come true. He said, "He bore the sins that we should have borne. He was wounded for our transgressions." (Isaiah 53:4-5) The Cost of Following Jesus
Jesus saw the crowds surrounding him. He ordered his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then a teacher came to heal him. He said, "My teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered and said, "The mouse has its holes, and the bird its nest; but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay His head."
Another follower said to him, "Lord, first let me go and bury my father."
And Jesus sought him saying, "Follow me." "And let the crowds protect the dead from their bodies." Then Jesus came to a lake of Galilee, and Jesus called for the wind to be held, and the sea became calm and the wind was stilled, and it became quiet.
Jesus entered a boat. His disciples followed him.
Suddenly, a fierce storm came upon the lake. The waves beat against the boats, but Jesus was sleeping.
The disciples woke him up. "Lord, we perish!" they cried. "We are perishing, we are dying, O Lord!"
He answered and said, "You of little faith! Why are you so afraid?" And He got up and rebuked the winds and the sea. And there was a great calm.
The disciples were amazed. They asked, "What kind of man is this? Even the winds and waves obey him!" Jesus heals two demon-possessed men.
Jesus arrived at the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gerasenes. A man from the city was there, who had been possessed by a legion of demons. The demons had often seized him, and he would be overcome and wander among the tombs near the sea. Seeing Jesus from a distance, the man ran and knelt before him. He shouted, "What do you want with me, Jesus, Son of the Most High God? I beg you, do not torment me!" For Jesus had been commanding the unclean spirit to come out of the man. The man who had been possessed by the legion of demons was sitting, clothed and in his right mind. The crowds who had witnessed the miracle told by the man who had been possessed. The people were all so terrified that they asked Jesus to leave their region. As Jesus was boarding the boat, the man who had been possessed by the legion of demons begged Jesus, "Allow me to accompany you." However, Jesus replied, "No, go home to your family and tell them how much the Lord has done for you, and how he has had mercy on you." So the man went away and began to publicly announce throughout the region of the Gerasenes all that Jesus had done for him.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried out. "Have you come here so that punishment will befall us before our time for reckoning is due?"
Not far away, a large group of pigs was eating.
The demons pleaded to Jesus, saying, "If you cast us out, thrust us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. Then the entire herd rushed down the steep hillside, rushed into the lake, and were drowned.
Those who were tending the pigs fled. They went to the city and reported this matter. They told the people what had happened to the men who were under the control of the demons.
So the people of the country came out in a body to meet Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged him to withdraw from their district.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
Jesus entered a boat. Then he crossed to the other side of the lake and arrived at his own town.
Some men brought to him a man who was paralyzed and could not speak. He was lying on a mat. Seeing their faith, Jesus said to the paralytic, "Take courage, son; your sins are forgiven."
Then one of the teachers of those who wronged themselves said, "This man is saying something very shameful!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. So he said, "Why do you have evil thoughts in your hearts?
Is it easier to say, "Your sins are forgiven," or to say, "Stand up and walk"?
You should know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then He spoke to the man who could not walk, saying, "Rise, take up your bed and walk to your house."
The man got up and entered his house.
When the Horse saw this, they were filled with wonder. So they praised God for a blessing that is among the greatest that He has given to His servants in terms of power. Jesus chooses Matthew.
As Jesus was going away from there, he saw a man named Matthew. He was sitting in the tax office. Jesus said, "Follow me." And Matthew got up and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having supper at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and sinners, the "undesirables," had come to eat with them. They were all having supper with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this; and they sent their disciples to Him, and said, "Why does your Teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this. And he said, "The ones who are well do not need a doctor, but the ones who are sick do need one."
Go and learn what this means: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice." (Hosea 6:6) I did not come to take those who believe they are right with God to follow me. I came to take the fallen to follow me. Jesus is asked about fasting.
One day, Jesus' disciples came to him. They said to him, "We and the Pharisees do not fast. Why then do your disciples also not eat?"
Jesus answered and said, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? But the days will come when the bridegroom will be taken away from them, and then they will fast."
People do not stick a new piece of cloth on old clothes. This new piece will come off the old clothes, making the scratch even worse.
Do not pour the new into an old train. And if they do, the train will explode. And the wine will flow, and the train will be destroyed. No, but everyone pours the new into a new train. And both are preserved. A deceased woman and a tortured woman.
While Jesus was saying these things, a man came and knelt down before Jesus. He said, "My daughter has just died. But come and lay your hand on her, and she will live again."
Jesus rose and followed them. His disciples also followed them.
Suddenly, a woman approached behind Jesus. She had suffered from a disease that caused constant bleeding for twelve years. She touched the edge of his robe.
"I have only to touch his clothes, and I shall be cured," I thought.
Jesus turned and saw her. He said, "Woman, do not despair." "Your faith has healed you." The woman was healed at that moment.
When Jesus entered the ruler's house, he saw the harp players there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go away. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the people were sent outside, Jesus entered. He took the girl by her hands, and she straightened up.
News of what Jesus had done spread everywhere in that region. Jesus heals two blind men.
As Jesus was leaving there, two blind men followed him, crying out, "Have mercy on us, O Lord, Son of David!"
When Jesus went inside, the deaf man came to him. He asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" They said, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes. He said, "It will happen to you as you believed."
Now they can see again. Jesus warned them sternly, saying, "Make sure no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. And they talked about it everywhere in that area.
As they were coming out, another man was brought to Jesus. He was possessed by a demon, and the demon had control over him. He was not able to speak.
When the devil was cast out, the man spoke. The multitude marveled, saying, "We never saw anything like this in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the prince of demons." There are few workers.
Jesus passed through all the cities and villages. He taught in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom of God. He healed every disease and affliction.
When he saw the crowds, he was deeply concerned for them. They were burdened and beaten, weak, like a sheep without a shepherd.
Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few."
Ask the Lord of the plow to send carpenters to His plowed field.
Jesus sent to the two tribes, twelve.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to come to him. And he gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease and sickness.
These are the names of the twelve apostles. The first of them is Simon son of John, and his brother Andrew. Then comes John son of Zebedee, and his brother James.
After that come Philip and Bartholomew, as well as Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. There is also James, the son of Alphaeus, and Thaddaeus.
Their last was Simon the Magician and Joses. Joses is the one who will later deliver Jesus to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve with the following command: "Do not go among the gentiles," he said. "Do not enter any city of the decapolis."
Instead, speak to the people of Israel. They are like lost herds.
While you are walking, proclaim this message: "The Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
Heal the sick. Raise the dead. Cleanse from the torment of the whip. Exorcise demons. You have been given a gift, so give a gift.
Do not bring gold or silver or copper as money belts.
Do not pack a bag for the trip. Do not take extra clothes, shoes, or a walking stick. What is needed should be given to the worker.
When you enter a town or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay in that person's home until you leave.
When you enter the house, you are welcome to those who live in it.
If that house welcomes you, then bless it with peace. And if it does not welcome you, then do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to your words. And if they do not, then shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or that city.
What I will mention is the truth. On the Day of Reckoning, it will be easier for the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I send you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be wise as serpents and harmless as doves.
Beware! They will hand you over to the local courts. They will flog you in their homes.
You will be grouped with wise and princely people because of my shenanigans. And you will witness them, and those who are not Jews.
But when you are arrested, do not worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, the appropriate words will be given to you to say.
You will not speak. The spirit of your father will speak through him.
Brothers will deliver brothers to be killed. And fathers will deliver their sons. And sons will rise up against their fathers and be killed.
Everyone will hate you for my sake. But whoever stands firm in their faith to the end will be saved.
When people attack you in a place, flee to another place. What I am about to tell you is the truth. You will not finish your journey through the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is not superior to his teacher, and the servant is not superior to his master.
It is sufficient for the student to be like his teacher. And it is sufficient for the slaves to be like their masters. And if the head of the household is called Beelzebub, what can the others who live in it expect?
Do not fear your enemies. For what is secret will be shown openly. And what is hidden will be revealed.
What you say in the dark, speak in the light. What is whispered in your ear, shout from the rooftops.
Do not be afraid of the destroyer of the body, even if it does not kill the soul. But rather fear the one who destroys both the soul and the body in Gehenna.
Isn't he telling you from Shiroto, that there are eight birds sold for one number of a leg? But one of them does not fall to the ground unless with the knowledge of your father?
He even counts every single hair on your head!
Do not be afraid. You are worth more than many birds.
What about the one who claims to know me in front of people? I will also say in front of my Father in heaven that I know him.
But what about the one who claims before the people that he does not know me? I will say before my Father who is in heaven that he does not know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to create "a discord between children and their parents. The daughter will disobey her mother. And there will be rivalries between stepmothers and their mothers."
A man's enemies will be from among the members of his own family.
He who loves his father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. And he who loves his son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And he who does not bear his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
Whoever has mercy on you, has mercy on me. And whoever has mercy on me, has mercy on the one who sent me.
If one receives a prophet like the Prophet, he will receive the reward of the Prophet. And if one receives a righteous person like the Righteous, he will receive the reward of the Righteous.
If anyone gives someone even a cup of cold water, a child who follows me, then what I will tell you is a fact. That person will certainly be rewarded.
Jesus and the prophet Yahya.
Jesus completed his teaching of his twelve disciples. Then he began to teach and preach in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was to come, or should we look for someone else?"
Jesus answered, "Go back to John and tell him what you have heard and what you have seen."
The blind are given sight, the disabled walk, those with skin diseases are healed, the deaf hear, the dead are raised, and the good news is preached to the poor.
Honor the people of faith who do not despair and do not abandon their faith because of me.
While John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John. He said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed swayed by the wind?"
If it is not that, then what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothing? No. For those who dress in fine clothing are standing in the palaces of kings.
What then did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, he told you and more than a prophet.
He is the one who was mentioned in the holy books. And it is said: "I will send my messenger before you, to make straight your way." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am going to tell you is the truth. No one has ever been born who is greater than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is greater than he is.
From the days of John the Baptist, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing, and powerful men are seizing it.
Prophets and the law spoke of the revelation until John came.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is the Eliadah who was supposed to come.
Whoever enjoys a good hearing should listen.
What can I compare today's people to? They are like children sitting in the markets and shouting at others, saying,
We played for you a tune on the flute, but you did not dance. We sang a funeral song, but you were not grieved.
When John came, he did not eat or drink as you drink. And people say, "He is bewitched."
And when the Son of Man came, He ate and drank with you. And the people said, "This man is always eating and drinking too much. He is a friend of tax collectors and 'sinners.'" And the ones who work to understand prove that understanding is right. Cities that refuse to accept sin
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where He had performed most of His miracles. The people of those cities did not repent of their sins. And He said:
How hard it is for you, Chorazin! How hard it is for you, Bethsaida! And if the mighty works had been done in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes. They would have sat down in mourning.
But I tell you this. On the day of reckoning it will be easier for the city of Tortosa and of Sidon than it will be for you.
And what about you, Capernaum? Will you be lifted up to heaven? No! But you will be brought down to the place of the dead. If the miracles that were done in you had been done in the city of Tyre, it would still be existing today.
But I tell you this. On the day of reckoning it will be easier for Sodomites than for you. A rest for all who are weary.
At that time Jesus said, "I praise you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and learned, and revealed them to little children.
Yes, Dad. This is what you wanted.
My father gave me everything. The father is the only one who teaches the son. The only ones who teach the father are the son and those whom the son has chosen to introduce him to.
Come to me all you who are weary and burdened, for you shall find rest for your souls.
Become my slaves and learn from me. I am gentle and not haughty. You will find rest for your souls.
My service is easy, and my downloads are light.
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath day, Jesus was walking through the wheat fields. His disciples were hungry. So they began to break off some heads of wheat and eat.
The Pharisees saw this; and they said to the Lord Christ, "Look! This man is transgressing the law by doing this on the Sabbath day. But your disciples are doing it too!"
Jesus answered, saying, "Has it not been told you what David and those with him did? They were hungry."
He entered the house of God. He and his entourage ate the holy bread. No one except the priests was allowed to eat it.
Have you not read the law? For it is written how the priests in the temple are bound to minister, and to execute their priestly office on that day, every sabbath. Yet they are not esteemed guilty.
I tell you that someone who is greater and more important than the temple is present.
The Bible says: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice." (Hosea 6: 6) You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not have filed a lawsuit against innocent people.
The Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath.
After that, Jesus went to their church.
There was a man who had a withered and crooked hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they asked him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep fell into a pit on the Sabbath? Wouldn't you lift it up and bring it out?"
Man is more precious than sheep! Therefore, the law allows us to do good work on the Sabbath.
Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and it was restored, just as sound as the other hand.
But the Pharisees were compelled to go out and they plotted to kill Jesus. Abdullah the Chosen One.
Jesus knew all the plans the Pharisees had for him. So he left that place. A great crowd followed him, and he cured all their sick.
But he warned them not to reveal his identity.
This was to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah had said:
This is my servant. I have chosen him. He is the one I love. I am very pleased with him. I will put my spirit upon him. And it shall come to pass for the salvation of the nation that all things shall be well.
He will argue nor shout. No one will hear him in the streets.
He will not break a broken branch. He will not extinguish a dying flame. He will restore everything to its rightful place.
Nations will put their hope in him. Jesus and Beelzebub.
A man possessed by demons, who was blind and unable to speak, was brought to Jesus. Jesus healed his disease. Then the man regained his ability to speak and see.
Everyone was astonished. They said, "Is this the son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow drives out demons by the prince of demons."
Jesus knew what they were thinking. He said to them, "Every kingdom that is divided against itself will be ruined; and every city or house that is divided against itself will not stand."
If a demon casts out a demon, it is fighting against itself. So how can its kingdom stand?
She says I compel demons with the power of Beelzebub. By what power do your people cast out demons? So, they will be your rulers.
But if the demons are cast out by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or think about it. How can you get into a powerful man's house and take what he owns? You have to tie him up first. Then you can tame his house.
He who does not support me is my enemy. And he who does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
Here is what I say to you: Every sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven; but speaking evil things against the Holy Spirit there is no forgiveness.
Whoever blasphemes the Son of Man, it shall be forgiven him. But whoever blasphemes against the Holy Spirit, it shall not be forgiven him. Such blasphemers will never be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If you do good to a tree, its fruit will be good. And if you do evil to a tree, its fruit will be evil. For you can know a tree by its fruit.
O you, nest of the toxic breeze! How dare you, enemies, how dare you speak well with your wicked hearts? Your mouths testify to what is within them.
A good companion says what is pleasant. This is because of the goodness which is combined in his essence. A bad companion says what is harmful. This is because of the evil which is combined in his essence.
But here is what I say to you. On the day of reckoning, people will have to meet every careless word they whispered.
By your words you will be condemned or justified. The wonderful sign of Jonah.
Some Pharisees and the teachers of the law came to Jesus and said, "Teacher, we want to see a miraculous sign from you."
He answered: "Indeed, the sinful and defiant nation asks for a wondrous sign! But you will not be given a sign except the sign of Yunus (peace be upon him)."
John was in the belly of a huge fish for three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The men of Nineveh will stand on the day of reckoning with the people living today. And the men of Nineveh will contend that these people are guilty. The men of Nineveh turned away from their sins when Jonah preached to them. And now, one greater than Jonah has come.
The Southern Queen will raise her voice on the day of reckoning with the Earth residents. And she will prove them guilty. She has come from a far distance to hear the wisdom of Solomon. And now, who is more important than Solomon is here.
What happens when a demon leaves a man? It travels to desolate places, searching for a place of rest. But it does not find it.
Then he says, "I will return to the house I left." And when he gets there, he finds the house empty. The house has been completely cleaned and tidied up.
Then the evil demon goes and takes with him seven other spirits more wicked than he is. They enter and stay there. This man is in a worse state than before. This is how it is with the wicked people of our time. But the Messiah and His brothers...
While Jesus was still speaking to the crowd, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to speak to him.
Someone told him: "Your mother and brother are standing outside. They want to talk to you."
And in Jesus' peace be upon him answer, he said: "Who is my mother? And who are my brothers?"
Jesus saw his disciples. He said, "This is my mother! These are my brothers!"
Whoever does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother and sister and mother.
The Farmer's Tale
On the same day, Jesus went out of the house and sat on the shore of the Sea of Galilee.
A large crowd gathered around him, so he got into a boat. He lay inside it, while everyone stood on the shore.
Then he told them many things based on stories. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds."
He threw the seed on the ground. Some of it fell on a path. Birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there wasn't much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun rose, the plants ignited. They dried out because they had no roots.
Some seeds remained, falling among the impurities. The impurities became saturated and overcame the plants.
Some seed also fell on good ground, and produced a crop that was many times larger, reaching a hundredfold, or sixtyfold, or thirtynfold of what was sown.
Whoever enjoys a good hearing should listen.
The students came to him. And they asked him, "Why do you use parables when you speak to people?"
He answered, saying: "You have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. This has not been withheld from those who are from without."
Whoever possesses this kind of knowledge will be given even more. Indeed, they will receive a very great deal. As for whoever does not possess this kind of knowledge, even what little they have will be taken away.
This is why I use parables when I speak to people. I say, "They look, but they don't really see. And they listen, but they don't understand or really listen."
In it the words of the prophet Isaiah are embodied. He said: "You will hear but not understand. You will see but not know what you see."
The hearts of these people have clung fast. They barely hear with their ears. They have closed their eyes. Otherwise, they would see with their own eyes, and hear with their own ears, and understand with their own hearts. And they would call upon their Lord, and He would heal them.
But God bless your eyes, for they see. And a curse upon your ears, for they hear.
What I will mention is a reality. Many of the prophets and the righteous ones wanted to see what you see. But they did not see. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear.
Listen up! This is the meaning of the peasant's story.
People hear the message about the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the devil comes. So he steals what was planted in their hearts. These people are like a seed that was planted on a road.
Some of them received the seed that fell on rocky places. These are the ones who hear the message and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no roots. Therefore, they do not last long. As soon as they face hardship or persecution because of the message, they quickly turn away from the faith.
While others received the seed that fell on rocks. They are the ones who hear the message. But then, the worries of the world and illusory gains intervene to obscure it. Preventing it from producing.
But others received the seed that fell on good ground. They are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce a crop, a hundred, sixty, or thirty times what the farmer planted. The story is about the weeds.
Jesus said to a crowd, "This is how the Kingdom of Heaven is. He told them another parable and said, 'A man planted a good seed in a field.'
While everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's servants came to them. They said, "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where did these weeds come from?"
He said, "An enemy did that," then they asked the servants, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"No," the owner answered. "While you are pulling out the weeds, you might pull out the wheat along with it."
Let both grow together until the harvest. And at that time I will say to the workers what they should do. And this is what I will tell them: First, gather the weeds. And bind them into bundles to be burned. Then gather the wheat. And bring it into my granary." "Tales of the Black Seed and the Yeast"
Jesus told the crowd another story. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. Suddenly, someone took the seed and planted it in a field."
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it is the largest of all the plants in the garden. It becomes a tree. And the birds come to rest in its branches.
Jesus told them another parable. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast." He said, "A woman took yeast and mixed it into a large amount of flour. The yeast worked its way through the entire dough."
Jesus said all these things to the crowds in parables. He did not tell them anything without a parable.
When the words of the Prophet came, they stirred up the truth. He said, "I will open my mouth and tell tales. I will speak of things that have been hidden since the creation." (Nusus 78:2) Jesus explains the story of the weeds.
Then Jesus went out from the crowd and entered a house. His disciples came to him and said, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered, saying: "Whoever sows good, from it will sprout."
The realm is the world. And the good oil symbolizes the people who belong to the kingdom. And the haters are the people who belong to Satan.
The devil is the one who sows them. And the harvest is the Day of Resurrection. And the workers are the angels.
The link is clarified and despised in the fire. Thus it will be on the Day of Resurrection.
The Son of Man will send his messengers. And they will purify his kingdom of all that causes sin. And they will remove all the wicked ones.
They will immerse them in a scorching blaze. There, the righteous will weep, and they will gnash their teeth in fear.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in the kingdom of his father. And those who have heard, let them hear. Tales of the hidden treasure and the pearl.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again, and in his joy he went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant in search of fine pearls.
He found something that was of great value. So he went and sold all that he had. And he bought that pearl. The story of the net.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the lake, and it gathered every kind of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen pulled it to the shore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish in baskets. But they threw the bad fish in the garbage.
This is how it will be on the Day of Judgment. The angels will come. They will separate those who did what is evil from those who did what is right.
The demons will be scorched in the hellfire, where they will writhe and weep.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes," they answered.
He told them, "Every scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a house, who brings out from his storeroom both new and old treasures." An unacknowledged prophet.
Jesus finished telling these stories. Then he left there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began teaching the people in their synagogue. They were amazed at his teaching. "Where did this man get this wisdom? And where did he get this power to perform miracles?" they asked.
Isn't this the son of the artisan? Isn't his mother called Mary? Aren't his brothers called John, Joseph, Simon and Jude?
Isn't every single one of his sisters with us? So what is this man obtaining all these things for?
They were not at all satisfied with him. Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own village and in his own house."
He only showed a few signs there because they did not believe.
John the Baptist's head was cut off.
At that time, Herod, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod had arrested John. He had bound him and imprisoned him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod's brother, Philip.
John was saying to Howard, "It is haram for you to possess her."
Romeo wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced before Herod and his guests. And Herod was exceedingly pleased with her.
He promised her his days, offering to give her everything she asked for.
Her mother told her what she should say. So the girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very angry. But he remembered his promise and the dinner guests. So he asked one of his men to give her what she had asked for.
Herod had John beheaded in prison.
They raised his head on a large platter and gave him the girl. Then she took him to her mother.
The disciples came and sat on his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to Ahmad. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The executioners heard about this, so they followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus came down to the shore, he saw large crowds. He was deeply concerned for them. So he healed the sick among them.
When the sun was almost set, the disciples came to him. They said, "Nothing is here. It is time to send the crowds away. Let them go into the villages and buy food for themselves."
Jesus answered and said, "They need not go away; give them something to eat."
We have nothing but five pounds of bread and only two small fish, they answered.
Bring them to me, he said.
Then Jesus instructed the people to sit down on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and gave thanks. Then he broke the loaves into pieces. After that, he gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. The disciples had gathered 12 baskets of leftover food.
There were about 5000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
Immediately after this, Jesus had the disciples lower the boat into the water. He instructed them to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee first, and then He sent the crowds away.
After he sent them, he went up alone to a mountain to pray to God. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The boat had moved a considerable distance from the shore. The waves were hitting it hard due to the winds blowing upon it.
In the early morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the water.
They saw him walking on the lake, and they trembled. "It's a ghost!" they cried. Then they screamed in fear.
Jesus said, "Believe that you yourselves are! I am He. Do not be afraid."
"Lord, if it is you," Peter asked, "tell me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." Simaa stepped out of the boat and walked on the water toward Jesus.
And when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. And he began to sink. So he cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Immediately, Jesus reached out his hand and held him. "Your faith is too small!" he said. Then he asked, "Why did you doubt Me?"
When they got into the boat, the wind died down.
They were filled with awe at Jesus and the Disciples. They said, "You are certainly the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and plunged into my Gensou-ryou.
The men who used to live there recognized Jesus. So they spread the message throughout the surrounding area. And the people brought all their sick to Jesus.
It was a relief to him when permission was given to the sick to simply touch the edge of his cloak. And everyone who was touched by it was healed.
What makes people "dirtier"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. They said,
Why do your pupils not obey what the elders teach them? Your pupils do not wash their hands before eating!
On what basis do you not obey God's command? Do you not prefer to follow your own instructions rather than that?
The Lord said, "You shall revere your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Leviticus 5:16) He also said, "Whoever curses his father or his mother shall be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to tell their parents: 'Whatever help you may have received from us is a separate gift to God.'
They do not need to honor their parents with their gifts. And you make the word of God void for the sake of your traditions.
Rebels! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said:
They greet me with their words, but their hearts are far from me.
Their worship is meaningless to me. They teach nothing but human laws.
Jesus called the multitudes. He said, "Listen, and understand."
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." It is what comes out of your mouth that makes you "unclean."
So the disciples came to them. And they asked, saying, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, saying, "There are plants my heavenly Father has not planted, and their roots will be pulled up."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. And if a blind man guides a blind man, they will both fall into a pit.
Peter said: "Explain this to us."
Haven't you understood enough already? Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Whatever goes into the mouth, goes into the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But what comes out of the mouth comes from the heart. These are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts proceed from the heart. So do murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. So do theft, false witness, and lying.
These are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A Canaanite woman who lived near Tyre and Sidon came to him, weeping. She fell at his feet and begged him, saying, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! A demon is torturing my daughter. She is suffering greatly."
Jesus did not utter a single word. Then his disciples came to him. They pleaded with him, "Send her away, for she keeps crying out after us."
Jesus answered and said, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel."
Then the woman fell on her knees before him. "Lord," she said, "have mercy on me!"
He said: "It is not right to take the bread of children and throw it to their dogs."
Yes, Lord, she said. "Even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' tables."
Then Jesus answered and said, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be as you desire." And her daughter was healed at that moment. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus left there. Then He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then He went up to a mountain and came down.
A large crowd came to him. They brought those who were blind in one eye and those who were lame in the feet. They also brought those who were crippled, those who were blamed, those who were weak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. And those who did not speak were speaking. And the dwarfs fixed. And the weak walked. And the blind saw. So the people cried out to the gods of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him. He said, "I have deep concern for these people. They have been with me for three days already, and they have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. And if I do, they will be too weak on their way back."
His disciples answered and said to them, "There is nothing here," and they said, "Where can we find enough to satisfy these great crowds?"
How many loaves of bread do you have?" Jesus asked. "Seven," they replied, "and a few small fish."
Jesus told the crowd to sit on the ground.
Taking the seven loaves and fish, He gave thanks. Then breaking them, He distributed them to His disciples to evangelize. And at that time, the disciples distributed them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. And afterward, the disciples collected seven jars of the leftovers.
The number of men who ate food was 4,000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he went up into a boat. Then he went to the region near Magadan.
Jesus is asked about a wondrous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
He answered, saying: "In the evening, you look at the sky. You say: 'The weather will be good. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning you say, "Today will be stormy. The sky is red and overcast." You know what you see in the sky means. But you do not understand the signs of what is happening now.
People are stabbing and going through narrow passages, searching for a wondrous sign. But no sign will be given to them except the sign of Jonah. Then Jesus left them and went away. The bread of the Pharisees and Sadducees is the bread of the sinners.
The students came to the other side of the lake. They had forgotten to take bread.
Pay attention, Jesus said to them. "Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils talked among themselves about this. They said, "He must say this because we did not bring any bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. And he said, "Your faith is very small! Why are you talking to each other about having no bread?
Have you not understood yet? Have you not remembered the five loaves of bread for the five thousand? Have you not remembered how many vessels you collected the pieces in?
Do you not remember the hundred loaves of bread for the four thousand? Do you not remember the quantity of vessels you gathered?
How do you not understand this in the first place? I did not speak to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus had not permitted them to observe the leaven used in bread. Rather, He was warning them about what they were being taught by the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter said, "Jesus is the Christ."
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. And there he asked his disciples, "Who do people say that the Son of Man is?"
They answered, "Some say the prophet Jonah; others say Elijah; and still others say Baruch, or some other prophet."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Replying, Simeon said, "You are the Messiah. You are the Son of the living God."
Jesus said, "Blessed are you, Simon, son of Jonah! For this was not revealed to you by flesh and blood, but by my Father who is in heaven."
This is what I tell you. You are Peter. I will build my church upon this rock. The iron gates of hell shall not be strong enough to destroy it.
I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks of his coming death.
From that time Jesus began to explain to His disciples what was going to happen to Him. He told them that He must go to Jerusalem, where He must suffer at the hands of the elders, the chief priests, and the scribes. He must be killed, and then He must rise from the dead on the third day.
Peter took Jesus to one side and began to rebuke him. "Never, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Then Jesus turned around and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. You are not thinking about the things of God, but about the things of people."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, "Whoever desires to follow me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What good is it for someone to gain the whole world and yet lose their soul? Or what can someone give in exchange for their soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father, and His angels will come with Him. And He will reward each person according to their deeds.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. And among those who are standing here, no one will die before they have seen the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Jesus' appearance was changed.
After six days, Jesus took Simon, Peter, James, and John the brother of James with him. He brought them up on a high mountain. There they were all alone.
There before him his appearance changed. His face shone like the sun. And his clothes became white as the light of light.
Suddenly, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here; if you wish, I will make three tabernacles, one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
While Peter was still speaking, a white cloud came down and enveloped them. Then they heard a voice from the cloud saying, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased. Listen to him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were afraid. They prostrated themselves with their faces to the ground.
But Jesus came and touched them. He said, "Get up, and do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw only Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. And on the way down, Jesus told them what they must do. He said, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man has been raised from the dead."
The disciples asked, saying, "Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus said, "It is true. He must come to me to make all things new."
But I tell you, Elijah has already come. They did not recognize him. And they did to him whatever they wanted. In the same way, they will make the Son of Man suffer.
Then the disciples realized that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who was suffering from Dropsy.
As they approached the good man, a man approached Jesus. He fell prostrate before him.
Oh Lord, have mercy on my son. He is shaking violently and enduring great suffering. He often falls into fire or water.
I delivered it to your disciples. But they were not able to heal him.
O people, unbelievers, cursed ones! said Jesus. "How long shall I tolerate you? How long shall I bear with you? Show me the child."
Jesus commanded the devil to depart from the body, and he departed. And the child was healed in that very instant.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately. And they asked, saying, "Why could we not cast out the demons?"
He answered, "Because your faith is too small. What I will tell you is the truth. If your faith was the size of a mustard seed, it would be enough. You could say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
